WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.768 --> 00:00:02.310
SPEAKER: So far in
this series you've

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.170
queried data stored
within BigQuery

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.120
by following a common path
of ingesting your data,

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.630
storing it, and
then analyzing it.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.540
But it's also possible to
query data stored externally

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.190
or outside of BigQuery.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.320
In today's episode of
"BigQuery Spotlight"

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.730
we're diving in to these
external data sources.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:25.802
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.802 --> 00:00:26.770
Wait.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:29.130
So the world exists
outside of BigQuery?

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:30.630
Of course it does.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.520
The good news is that
it's possible to leave

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.810
your data in place and use
BigQuery as your query engine.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:40.470
We call these sources external
or federated data sources.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.510
This functionality is
currently supported

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.990
for data residing in Google
Drive, Cloud Storage, Cloud

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.330
SQL, and Bigtable.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.450
Before we look closer at
how to query these sources,

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.240
let's discuss a few notable
differences you'll experience

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.040
with external data sources.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.100
First to note is that the query
performance for external data

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.920
sources may not equal the
query performance of data

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.750
stored in BigQuery.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.430
So if query speed is a
priority in your case,

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:11.060
you may want to load the
data directly into BigQuery.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.610
Secondly, when querying
an external source,

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:17.130
BigQuery cannot predict the
amount of data to be processed.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.850
So you will only know
after you run your query.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.050
Finally, the results
are not cached

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.100
like they would be when querying
data stored in BigQuery.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.160
Caching is a great way to save
costs and improve performance

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.130
on repeated queries when the
underlying data hasn't changed.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.460
So given these
considerations, what

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.970
are some popular use cases
for external data sources?

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.640
Overall, this feature
is best for short term,

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.110
less frequently accessed data.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.770
For example, you could
use external data sources

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:48.050
to support loading and
transforming your data

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:50.320
in just a single pass.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.180
In this workflow, you
query the external source,

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.640
transform the data
as part of the query,

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.695
and then write the results
as a permanent table

00:01:57.695 --> 00:02:00.080
in BigQuery storage.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.510
Another use case is for
joining small amounts

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:06.230
of frequently changing data
with data stored in BigQuery.

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.210
By keeping the
frequently changing data

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.400
as an external data
source, it does not

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.890
need to be reloaded
into BigQuery every time

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:15.210
it is updated.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:18.690
An example here is querying data
that lives in a Google Sheet.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.330
Even as the Sheet is
edited in real time,

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:23.310
you can run queries
over the data

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.940
and the results will reflect the
live, up-to-date information.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:27.660
OK.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.420
So now let's look at the how
for setting up external data

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.820
sources in BigQuery.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.670
You can follow along in
your own BigQuery sandbox.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.290
If you don't have
one yet, set one up

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.450
following the video link below.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.400
In this example,
you'll run a query

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.490
over a collection of JSON files
located in a Cloud storage

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:45.630
bucket.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.670
These files have the
flight performance data

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:51.600
for all domestic flights in
the United States from 2014.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.360
Starting in the console,
create a new data set.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.550
Highlight your project
name in the left hand nav

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:59.120
and click Create Data Set.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.150
Name the data set
flight_performance,

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:06.750
choose US for the location,
and click Create Data Set.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:11.600
Now highlight your new data
set and click Create Table.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:14.930
Under Source, choose
Google Cloud Storage.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:18.110
Quick note, if your data was
located in Google Drive--

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:19.700
such as in a Google Sheet--

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.400
or in Bigtable, you would
choose those alternate sources

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.750
in the dropdown.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:25.610
The flight performance
data is located

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.190
in a public bucket
which any GCP user can

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:30.880
access with the URI address.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:32.960
Paste the URI provided
in the description

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:36.230
below into the GCS bucket field.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.900
You'll see there's a wild card
character in the URI, which

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.840
indicates to BigQuery to
include all the JSON files that

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.410
adhere to the specific
naming convention.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.600
Next, under Destination,
make sure you

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.100
set the table type to external.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.810
Name the table 2014.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.750
And you can auto detect
the schema in this case.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.840
Finally, click Create Table.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.030
Since there is no data
ingestion involved,

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.910
you'll see the table immediately
populate under the data set.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.440
Look at the table
details where you

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.780
can see the external
configuration that you just

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:16.260
set up and a table
size of 0 bytes

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:19.800
because your external table does
not use any BigQuery storage.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:23.370
Now you can run a query that
references the external table.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:26.250
In our query we're selecting
account of all flights

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.020
by carrier.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:30.840
As we discussed earlier, you
cannot see the amount of data

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.030
processed until after
the query completes.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.140
In this example, you created a
permanent for the external data

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:41.200
source.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.830
However, you can also
query an external source

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:47.430
using a temporary table which
is useful for one time ad hoc

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.490
queries or for ETL processes.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.800
More information on
how to create and query

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.500
temporary external
tables is linked below.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.780
Want to analyze
data in Cloud SQL?

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.630
Creating the initial connection
follows a different process

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:04.180
than you just saw for
Cloud Storage in Drive.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:07.090
But never fear, we've created
a quick start video link

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.980
below that shows
you end-to-end how

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.040
to connect to and query
your MySQL or Postgres

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.830
instance in Cloud SQL.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:18.220
Looking to data sources beyond
the Google Cloud ecosystem,

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:20.620
we recently announced
BigQuery Omni

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.390
which gives BigQuery
users the ability

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:25.390
to analyze data stored
in other public clouds.

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:27.160
Be sure to check out
the announcement blog

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.970
post link below.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.750
Thanks for joining us today
for "BigQuery Spotlight."

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:35.050
Watch out for our next episode
and remember, stay curious.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:43.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

